// The paper, and the order things happen in.
//
// The stage owns the black backdrop the terminal's white ink is on, and the
// green flag that starts the project. The order is the reference's, and it
// matters:
//
//   1. The terminal resets and clears its screen.
//   2. The terminal starts its own loop, which draws whatever the guest prints.
//      It is a broadcast and not a broadcast-and-wait because that loop never
//      ends.
//   3. The machine powers on -- which lays the kernel image into RAM and signs
//      on -- and waits, so that the first thing drawn is the boot message.
//   4. The machine starts running frames.
//
// The reference measures the stage with a 2 by 2 costume first. The terminal
// here is 64 columns by 20 rows of a 480 by 360 stage, which is a size the
// project fixes rather than one it asks for, so there is nothing to measure.

stage {
    costume "paper" = "assets/backdrop.svg";

    broadcast "Terminal.reset";
    broadcast "Terminal.run";
    broadcast "RISCV.powerOn";
    broadcast "RISCV.run";

    on flag_clicked {
        events::broadcast_and_wait("Terminal.reset");
        events::broadcast("Terminal.run");
        events::broadcast_and_wait("RISCV.powerOn");
        events::broadcast("RISCV.run");
    }
}
